<h3>Active stretching</h3>
<p>In active (static-active) stretching, you hold a stretch for 10 to 15 seconds using your own muscles, with no help from equipment or another person. Active stretching strengthens the working muscles while the opposing muscle group relaxes. It is similar to many yoga poses, and it never forces a muscle past what it can tolerate.</p>
<h3>Passive stretching</h3>
<p>In passive (static-passive) stretching, a partner or a piece of equipment helps you into and through the stretch. Passive stretching improves flexibility and can reduce muscle soreness, which makes it a good choice after a workout. It is also useful when you are recovering from an injury or cannot stretch on your own.</p>
