    'prolotherapy' => [
        'status' => 'published',
        'excerpt' => 'Dextrose prolotherapy injections designed to stimulate the body’s natural healing response in weak or injured ligaments, tendons and joints.',
        'hero_text' => 'A non-surgical injection option for weak or injured ligaments, tendons and joints, designed to stimulate your body’s own repair process.',
        'what_is' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Prolotherapy is short for "proliferation therapy." It is intended to stimulate the production of collagen, a protein the body needs to build the connective tissue that holds the skeleton together: tendons, ligaments, muscle fascia and joint capsules.</p>
<p>Prolotherapy is an injection of a mild irritant, most often dextrose (a simple sugar), into an area that is arthritic, worn or injured. The injection triggers a controlled inflammatory response. That may seem counterintuitive, but inflammation is part of the body's natural healing process. It draws repair cells, including fibroblasts and cartilage-producing chondrocytes, to the area to rebuild the collagen matrix of the tissue.</p>
<p>Published studies have reported benefits of prolotherapy for a range of musculoskeletal conditions, including lateral epicondylosis (tennis elbow), rotator cuff tendinopathy, plantar fasciitis, Achilles tendinopathy, osteoarthritis, low back pain and sacroiliac joint pain.</p>
HTML,
        'conditions' => "Lower back or mid-back pain\nDegenerative disc disease\nSacroiliac joint instability or dysfunction\nNeck pain\nKnee pain and meniscal tears\nWrist or hand pain\nOsteoarthritis\nRotator cuff tears\nTennis elbow\nPlantar fasciitis\nAchilles tendinosis\nTMJ",
        'how_it_works' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Dextrose prolotherapy is a mixture of dextrose and lidocaine injected into weak or injured ligaments, tendons and joints. The injections cause a temporary, low-grade inflammation that starts the healing process. New collagen begins to form in the injured tissue and, as it matures, becomes stronger and tighter, helping the tendon or ligament withstand daily and sports activities.</p>
<p>Injections can be ultrasound-guided for precise placement. Before treatment, you will have an evaluation with Dr. Andre Silano to see whether prolotherapy is right for you.</p>
HTML,
        'benefits' => "Non-surgical option for tendon and ligament problems\nUses the body's own healing response\nMay reduce the need for long-term medication\nAvoids the tissue-weakening concerns associated with repeated cortisone injections\nLess invasive and less expensive than surgery",
        'what_to_expect' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The injection contains a local anesthetic to reduce discomfort. You may have mild swelling and stiffness afterward, which simple over-the-counter pain relievers such as acetaminophen can ease. Avoid anti-inflammatory drugs such as aspirin and ibuprofen, which suppress the healing response.</p>
<h3>Before and after treatment</h3>
<ul>
<li>Stop non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) one week before the procedure and throughout your course of treatment.</li>
<li>You should be at least three months past a cortisone injection and one week past oral steroids.</li>
<li>Ice or heat may be applied as needed.</li>
<li>After the first week, patients typically begin a rehabilitation program. Avoid aggressive activity.</li>
</ul>
