        'what_is' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Wharton's jelly is named after Thomas Wharton, the English physician who first described it in 1656. It is a gelatinous connective tissue in the umbilical cord that insulates and protects the cord. Once considered waste after birth, it has been widely studied by researchers and physicians.</p>
<p>The tissue comes from donated umbilical cords collected after healthy live births by C-section. Donors are prescreened for infectious disease, and the tissue is processed and screened again before it is frozen and shipped for use. Wharton's jelly may be used alone or together with your own platelet-rich plasma (PRP), as determined by your provider.</p>
